# 07 — Cautions

1. **Back up before any write.**
   - Save `/userdata/RobotData/{map,robot.db,active_map.json}` and, if you're experimenting with the VSLAM map, `/userdata/vslam`.
   - Deleting a map cascades to its tasks.
2. **Port 13344 has no authentication.**
   - The `mower_logic` HTTP API listens on all network interfaces.
   - Anyone on the same Wi-Fi, LTE or Tailscale network can list or delete maps and start the mower.
   - Consider a firewall rule such as `iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 13344 ! -s <your-ip> -j DROP`. Check it doesn't break the app first, since it may use this port locally.
   - The same applies to `foxglove_bridge` on 8765, and to the ROS master ports, though the master itself binds to 127.0.0.1.
3. **Blade safety.**
   - Remote-control mode can turn on the blade (`96` cutter=1, `/logic/cutter_control`).
   - Test drive commands with the blade off, ideally with the wheels off the ground.
   - The BLE link expects a heartbeat every 100 ms. A failsafe on heartbeat loss is likely but unconfirmed.
4. **OTA updates** (archives in `/userdata/RobotData/upgrade/`) likely replace `/workspace`, so swapped models, configs and edits may be lost. None of these interfaces are a public, stable API.
5. **Webshell agent.** The cloud can enable a remote shell agent (`Webshell_Enable_Req`). Be aware of this if you're thinking about the security of the device.
6. **What I checked and what I didn't.**
   - Verified on the device: the BLE framing (from source), the service types, the map storage, `/map/list` over HTTP, and the model details.
   - Taken from vendor docs or binary strings, **untested**: the other HTTP endpoints, the BLE IDs beyond the framing, and the MQTT topic composition.
====
# 09 — Reuse strategy: keep the drivers, replace the brain

You don't need a closed node's **source** to reuse it — only its **ROS interface**, and those
are open (`mower_msgs`: 88 defs; `mower_gps_msgs`: 11). The driver-layer binaries already turn
the proprietary hardware into clean ROS topics — which is exactly the work you'd otherwise
spend reverse-engineering the AT32 mainboard / dock / LoRa protocols (docs 02, 08).

So the pragmatic path isn't "rewrite everything" — it's **keep the ~7 hardware-abstraction
nodes, replace the ~20 algorithmic/decision nodes** you'd want to own anyway.

## Keep as-is (hardware abstraction layer)

| Node(s) | Wraps | Why reuse |
|---|---|---|
| `mower_base` (`base_driver_node`) | AT32 chassis MCU over `/dev/ttyUSB0` | motors, blade, bumper, IMU, odom, battery, buttons, LEDs — and it enforces the safety cutoffs (estop/bumper/lift). Reusing it **keeps that safety behaviour**. |
| `mower_charge` | dock detection + charge electronics | proprietary dock cues |
| `mower_gps_node` / `base_rtk` | Unicore UM960 + LoRa base-station link | proprietary LoRa pairing; gives you `/fix` |
| `base_cameras` / `stereo_ros` | Metoak stereo (MIPI/V4L2) | or skip and grab raw V4L2 frames yourself (camera is standard) |
| `base_keys`, `base_net`, `light_*` | buttons, connectivity, lighting | thin shims |

## Replace with your own open stack

| Stock (closed) | Open replacement |
|---|---|
| `det_ros` (YOLOv8) | your retrained YOLO on the NPU (doc 03) |
| `seg_ros` | your segmentation model |
| `vslam_ros`, `stereo_imu_tracking`, `*_localization` | OpenVINS / VINS-Fusion / ORB-SLAM3 + `robot_localization` EKF |
| `navigation`, `*_planner`, `*_controller` | Nav2 (or ROS1 move_base) + your planners |
| `mower_logic`, `mower_bt_nodes` | your own BehaviorTree.CPP tree |
| `mower_map`, `mower_mqtt` | your map store + cloud link |

You drive the robot by publishing `/cmd_vel` and calling `/logic/cutter_control`, exactly as
the stock stack does.

## The contract to build against (verified on the running robot)

**Consume (subscribe):**
| Topic | Type | From |
|---|---|---|
| `/odom` | `nav_msgs/Odometry` | `mower_base` |
| `/imu` | `sensor_msgs/Imu` | `mower_base` |
| `/wheel_vel` | `geometry_msgs/TwistStamped` | `mower_base` |
| `/bumper_cloud` | `sensor_msgs/PointCloud2` | `mower_base` |
| `/battery` | `sensor_msgs/BatteryState` | `mower_base` |
| `/tf` | `tf2_msgs/TFMessage` | `mower_base` |
| `/fix` | GNSS fix | `mower_gps_node` |
| `/left_oa_camera/image_raw`, `/right_oa_camera/image_raw`, `/vio/*` | `sensor_msgs/Image` | stereo |
| `/mower_base/status`, `/mower_sensor_info`, `/notice_code` | `mower_msgs/*` | `mower_base` |

**Command (publish / call):**
| Interface | Type | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `/cmd_vel` | `geometry_msgs/Twist` | drive |
| `/logic/cutter_control` | `mower_msgs/CutterControl` srv | blade motor + height |
| `/clear_estop` | `std_srvs/Empty` | clear fault |
| `/mapping_control`, `/localization/{LoadMap,SaveMap,GetMapInfo}` | `mower_msgs/*` | maps (docs 05/06) |

## Two deployment shapes

1. **Stay on Noetic (simplest).** Keep the driver nodes, swap only the algorithmic nodes for
   your own ROS 1 nodes. No bridge, no OS upgrade. Fastest route to new perception/behaviour.
2. **Hybrid Noetic + ROS 2.** Keep the Noetic drivers, run your brain in a ROS 2 (Humble)
   container, join with `ros1_bridge` — bridge only the contract topics above. More moving
   parts (build the bridge, include `mower_msgs`), higher CPU for image bridging.

Either way the closed binaries **must keep a Noetic / Ubuntu 20.04 / aarch64 runtime** with
their vendor libs — they can't be dropped onto a bare ROS 2 system.

## Caveats

- **Don't disable the safety nodes.** `mower_base` is the thing enforcing estop/bumper/lift —
  keep it in the loop.
- **OTA overwrites `/workspace`.** Pin/disable updates or clone the rootfs before you build on
  top; otherwise an update reverts your changes.
- **Don't `apt upgrade`** to "modernize" first — it would replace the ROS libs the closed
  binaries link against, risking an ABI break with no way to recompile them (doc 07 / the apt
  note). Patch targeted if you must.
- **Licensing:** reusing the vendor binaries unmodified on your own device is generally fine;
  redistribution is where the GPL/AGPL questions in doc 08 apply.
